   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Five tones, four tone marks",
     "texto": [
      "The five tones are **mid**, **low**, **falling**, **high** and **rising**. There are four tone marks: **่**, **้**, **๊** and **๋**. The mark does not always give the same tone on its own: the class of the initial consonant also matters."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Live syllable and dead syllable",
     "texto": [
      "A syllable is usually **live** if it ends in a long vowel or in a continuous sound such as m, n or ng. It is usually **dead** if it ends in a short vowel or in a p, t or k ending. With low-class consonants, dead syllables also tell a short vowel from a long one. Do not work out the tone by looking only at the mark."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Pairs to train your ear",
     "texto": [
      "**ไก่** (chicken) and **ไข่** (egg) share the mark **่**, but the initial consonant belongs to another class and changes the tone. **ไหม** (question particle) and **ไม่** (no) are written almost the same: the mark changes, and so do the tone and the meaning. Spelling and tone help you tell them apart."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
